{"records":[{"id":"9dk3rft75t","title":"Discovery and functional interrogation of SARS-CoV-2 RNA-host protein interactions. Flynn et al.","description":"Supplemental tables for the manuscript \"Discovery and functional interrogation of SARS-CoV-2 RNA-host protein interactions.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-27T00:29:06.984Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9dk3rft75t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9dk3rft75t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Julia","last_name":"Belk","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","CRISPR","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4958h9zkty","title":"SARS-CoV-2 ncrRNA interactome","description":"A deep understanding of SARS-CoV-2-host interactions is crucial to developing effective therapeutics and addressing the threat of emerging coronavirus. The role of non-coding regions of viral RNA (ncrRNAs) has not been systematically scrutinized. We developed a method using MS2 affinity purification coupled with liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (MAMS) to systematically map the interactome of SARS-CoV-2 ncrRNA in Calu-3, Huh7, and HEK293T cells. Integration of the results defined the core ncrRNA-host protein interactomes. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-31T14:08:30.152Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4958h9zkty","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4958h9zkty"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Liuyiqi","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"642gjvx74d","title":"Nonstructural protein 1 of SARS-CoV-2 is a potent pathogenicity factor redirecting host protein synthesis machinery toward viral RNA. Yuan et al","description":"Supplemental tables of \"Nonstructural protein 1 of SARS-CoV-2 is a potent pathogenicity factor redirecting host protein synthesis machinery toward viral RNA\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-19T14:44:13.43Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/642gjvx74d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/642gjvx74d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shuai","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7fgg5452pr","title":"The SARS-CoV-2 interactome","description":"SARS-CoV-2 is an RNA virus whose success as a pathogen relies on its abilities to repurpose host RNA-binding proteins (RBPs) and to evade antiviral RBPs. To uncover the SARS-CoV-2 RNA interactome, we here develop a robust ribonucleoprotein (RNP) capture protocol and identify 109 host factors that directly bind to SARS-CoV-2 RNAs. Applying RNP capture on another coronavirus HCoV-OC43 revealed evolutionarily conserved interactions between coronaviral RNAs and host proteins. Transcriptome analyses and knockdown experiments delineated 17 antiviral RBPs including ZC3HAV1, TRIM25, PARP12, and SHFL and 8 proviral RBPs such as EIF3D and CSDE1 which are responsible for co-opting multiple steps of the mRNA life cycle. This also led to the identification of LARP1, a downstream target of the mTOR signaling pathway, as an antiviral host factor that interacts with the SARS-CoV-2 RNAs. Overall, this study provides a comprehensive list of RBPs regulating coronaviral replication and opens new avenues for therapeutic interventions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-07T07:11:04.995Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7fgg5452pr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7fgg5452pr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sungyul","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0003-3207-1199"},{"first_name":"Young-suk","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0003-0220-9951"},{"first_name":"Yeon","last_name":"Choi","orcid":"0000-0003-4983-6573"},{"first_name":"Aheyon","last_name":"Son","orcid":"0000-0001-7945-3851"},{"first_name":"Youngran","last_name":"Park","orcid":"0000-0002-8621-1399"},{"first_name":"Kyung-Min","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0002-1373-5407"},{"first_name":"Jeesoo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jong-Seo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0002-8909-8440"},{"first_name":"V. Narry","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0002-3213-4965"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Proteomics","RNA","Interactome","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r52v2drd9b","title":"Aguilar-Pineda et al. Structural and functional analysis of female sex hormones against SARS-Cov-2 cell entry","description":"This dataset contains all the raw data and codes used for th adquision of the data presented in the manuscript: \"Structural and functional analysis of female sex hormones against SARS-Cov-2 cell entry \" by Aguilar-Pineda et al.\nHere we report new insights into the molecular basis of the interactions between the SARS-CoV-2 spike (S) protein and the human ACE2 receptor. We further report that glycosylation of the ACE2 receptor enhances SARS-CoV-2 infectivity. Importantly estrogens can disrupt glycan-glycan interactions and glycan-protein interactions between the human ACE2 and the SARS-CoV2 thereby blocking its entry into cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-11T13:04:31.536Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r52v2drd9b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r52v2drd9b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jorge Alberto","last_name":"Aguilar Pineda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karin J.","last_name":"Vera López","orcid":"0000-0002-7908-8230"},{"first_name":"Rita M.","last_name":"Nieto-Montesinos","orcid":"0000-0002-5671-4593"},{"first_name":"Gonzalo H. ","last_name":"Davila-Del Carpio","orcid":"0000-0001-7842-9286"},{"first_name":"Badhin","last_name":"Gómez Valdez","orcid":"0000-0001-6539-1207"},{"first_name":"Christian L. ","last_name":"Lino Cardenas","orcid":"0000-0001-5491-4375"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Structural Biology","Estrogen","Molecular Dynamics Study","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fcvjnp9mff","title":"Global analysis of protein-RNA interactions in SARS-CoV-2 infected cells reveals key regulators of infection. Kamel, Noerenberg, Cerikan et al","description":"Supplemental Spreadsheets to accompany \"Global analysis of protein-RNA interactions in SARS-CoV-2 infected cells reveals key regulators of infection.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-29T14:40:21.705Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fcvjnp9mff","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fcvjnp9mff"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Wael","last_name":"Kamel","orcid":"0000-0002-7625-2648"},{"first_name":"Marko","last_name":"Noerenberg","orcid":"0000-0002-4834-8888"},{"first_name":"Berati","last_name":"Cerikan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Honglin ","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aino","last_name":"Järvelin","orcid":"0000-0002-1225-4396"},{"first_name":"Mohamed ","last_name":"Kammoun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jeffrey","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ni","last_name":"Shuai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manuel","last_name":"Garcia Moreno","orcid":"0000-0001-7181-5459"},{"first_name":"Anna ","last_name":"Andrejeva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Deery","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Natasha","last_name":"Johnson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christopher","last_name":"Neufeldt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mirko","last_name":"Cortese","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Knight","orcid":"0000-0002-8780-1630"},{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Lilley","orcid":"0000-0003-0594-6543"},{"first_name":"Javier","last_name":"Martinez","orcid":"0000-0001-9152-7323"},{"first_name":"Ilan","last_name":"Davis","orcid":"0000-0002-5385-3053"},{"first_name":"Ralf","last_name":"Bartenschlager","orcid":"0000-0001-5601-9307"},{"first_name":"Shabaz","last_name":"Mohammed","orcid":"0000-0003-2640-9560"},{"first_name":"Alfredo","last_name":"Castello","orcid":"0000-0002-1499-4662"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences","Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"57j37g6kdh","title":"High-resolution photocatalytic mapping of SARS-CoV-2 spike interactions on the cell surface. Datta et al","description":"Identifying virus-host interactions on the cell surface can improve our understanding of viral entry and pathogenesis. SARS-CoV-2, the causative agent of the COVID-19 disease, uses ACE2 as a receptor to enter cells. Yet the full repertoire of cell surface proteins that contribute to viral entry is unknown. We developed a photocatalyst-based viral-host protein microenvironment mapping platform (ViraMap) to probe the molecular neighborhood of the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein on the human cell surface. Application of ViraMap to ACE2-expressing cells captured ACE2, the established co-receptor NRP1, and several novel cell surface proteins. We systematically analyzed the relevance of these candidate proteins to SARS-CoV-2 entry by knockdown and overexpression approaches in pseudovirus and authentic infection models and identified PTGFRN and EFNB1 as bona fide viral entry factors. Our results highlight additional host targets that participate in SARS-CoV-2 infection and showcase ViraMap as a powerful platform for defining viral interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-07T06:19:10.123Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/57j37g6kdh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/57j37g6kdh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mohsan","last_name":"Saeed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suprama","last_name":"Datta","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9rc2n4bkmv","title":"Functional interrogation of a SARS-CoV-2 host protein interactome identifies unique and shared coronavirus host factors. Hoffmann et al.","description":"The ongoing SARS-CoV-2 pandemic has devastated the global economy and claimed more than one million lives, presenting an urgent global health crisis. To identify host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and seasonal coronaviruses, we designed a focused high-coverage CRISPR-Cas9 library targeting 332 members of a recently published SARS-CoV-2 protein interactome. We leveraged the compact nature of this library to systematically screen SARS-CoV-2 at two physiologically relevant temperatures (33 ºC and 37 ºC) along with three related coronaviruses (HCoV-229E, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-OC43), allowing us to probe this interactome at a much higher resolution relative to genome scale studies. This approach yielded several new insights, including unexpected virus-specific differences in Rab GTPase requirements and GPI anchor biosynthesis, as well as identification of multiple pan-coronavirus factors involved in cholesterol homeostasis. This coronavirus essentiality catalog could inform ongoing drug development efforts aimed at intercepting and treating COVID-19, and help prepare for future coronavirus outbreaks.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T19:01:19.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9rc2n4bkmv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9rc2n4bkmv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9pmrdxdpyg","title":"\"Characterization and functional interrogation of SARS-CoV-2 RNA interactome. Labeau et al\"","description":"This dataset contains five supplemental tables from the manuscript by labeau et al. \" Characterization and functional interrogation of SARS-CoV-2 RNA interactome\".\n\nTable S1. Sequences of the 129 biotinylated ChIRP probes. Related to Figure 1.\n\nTable S2. Datasets of the mass spectrometry analyses. Related to Figure 1.\n\nTable S3. SAINTexpress analyses of the ChIRP-M/S dataset. Related to Figure 1.\n\nTable S4. List of RBP identified in ChIRP-M/S that overlap with other SARS-CoV-2 interactome. Related to Figure 1. \n\nTable S5. Cell viability assay of the siRNA screen. Related to Figure 4.\n ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-25T21:30:03.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9pmrdxdpyg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9pmrdxdpyg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"laurent","last_name":"Meertens","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zg7wp4xd5v","title":"SARS-CoV-2 disrupts splicing, translation and protein transport to evade host defenses: Banerjee et al.","description":"Enrichment values for interactions of SARS-CoV-2 viral proteins with host structural RNAs and messenger RNAs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-09-26T05:44:06.796Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zg7wp4xd5v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zg7wp4xd5v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Abhik","last_name":"Banerjee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Emily","last_name":"Bruce","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mario","last_name":"Blanco","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mitchell","last_name":"Guttman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dev","last_name":"Majumdar","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA","Binding"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":142757,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":142,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":142757,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":142012,"restricted":70,"embargoed":675},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":3608},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2293},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":698},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":469},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":413},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":391},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":323},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":275}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":140111},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":140},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":42},{"id":"7e51a2dd